{"kind":"report","text":"approved for head 7ab18c6be3fa54ad7bae46ac4565b21d9d89081e and all four artifacts.\n\nThe ratified changes-requested finding 99422578 is addressed, at the right place and in the right way. Both mutations that survived round 1 now fail. One residual observation is recorded below; it is not blocking and it is not what the finding asked for.\n\nRATIFIED FINDING 99422578 - CLOSED\n\nPoint of use, confirmed by reading the call graph rather than the claim. validateActorCount compares the decoded worker result with the case about to use it - result.ActorCount, which the worker reads from the fixture manifest, against selected.fixtureKey().actorCount - and not against the fixture's own directory name. The placement covers every decoded result because there is exactly one decode site: runWorker is a thin delegation to runWorkerDiagnostic, which decodes once and validates immediately after. Warm-ups, measured samples and the Trace2 diagnostic pass all funnel through it.\n\nCached-manifest rejection, confirmed. ensureFixtures still refuses a cached manifest whose actor count differs from its key, and that refusal is now pinned by a test that prepares a real one-actor fixture in a directory named actors-8 and requires the \"does not match its key\" error.\n\nMUTATION RE-RUN, the two that survived round 1\n\n(a) Drop \"|| manifest.ActorCount != key.actorCount\" from the cached-fixture guard: now RED, TestEnsureFixturesRejectsCachedActorCountMismatch fails. It was green at 3fff9596.\n(b) Make the fixture key ignore actor count, so every actor case shares the one-actor fixture: now RED on two tests, TestEnsureFixturesRejectsCachedActorCountMismatch and TestWorkerResultActorCountMustMatchCase. It was green at 3fff9596. I confirmed the edit landed by reading the diff before running, rather than trusting a failing grep.\n\nBoth were restored and the package re-verified green.\n\nRESIDUAL, NOT BLOCKING\n\nA third mutation I had not run in round 1 does survive: deleting the three-line validateActorCount call from runWorkerDiagnostic, while leaving the function itself intact, keeps the whole suite green. TestWorkerResultActorCountMustMatchCase pins the helper's logic, not the fact that anything calls it. So the binding is correct today and proven correct today, but a future edit that drops or bypasses the call would not be caught.\n\nI am not blocking on this, for a reason I checked rather than assumed: laneCommand, runWorker and runWorkerDiagnostic have no test coverage at all in this package and never have, because exercising them means building binaries and spawning workers. The uncovered call site is a pre-existing property of the lane orchestration, not something this head introduces, and closing it properly is a lane-level integration test rather than a repair to this change. Worth its own request if anyone wants the lane pinned; it should not hold this head. The narrower alternative, if it is ever wanted cheaply, is to derive wantActors in validateActorCount from max(selected.ActorCount, 1) directly instead of routing it back through selected.fixtureKey(), which would make the runtime guard independent of the key derivation it is guarding.\n\nFIVE CONDITIONS OF REQUEST 4a6f108a - STILL MET\n\nThe delta from my previously reviewed head 3fff9596 is exactly cmd/gitseq-perf/main.go and cmd/gitseq-perf/main_test.go, so the round 1 evidence for the five gaps carries over unchanged; I re-ran the parts that could regress. Depth reaches 500,000 and is runnable, measured in round 1 at 84.4s and 459MB for the full tier with linear scaling near 0.14 ms per record. Actor count is isolated from record count and I re-ran cold_status at actors 8: actor_count 8, 21 Git processes. Dependency fan-out is carried into the worker against exact prior bases and I re-ran submit_ack at fanout 64 depth 1000: dependency_fanout 64, 53 Git processes, with out-of-range fan-out still refused (\"dependency fan-out 64 must be between 1 and sample depth 50\"). Trace2 process count and duration are populated. Cold materialization still removes the checkpoint ref and pointer. The gated integration suite passes.\n\nARCHITECTURE\n\nPreserved, and no architecture-page update is required. The delta touches one CLI file and its test. cmd/gitseq-perf remains the only importer of internal/perfscenario and internal/perflane, so the fixture writer and its private-key parser stay unreachable from gs, the resident and the MCP binaries. No production package is touched anywhere in this branch: no internal/kernel, gitstore, app, service, workroom, nexus or statusview file appears in the diff against main. None of the measurement packages appear in the package table of docs/reference/architecture.md, and no listed layer's contract moves. My round 1 architecture conclusion stands, including the recorded drift risk that fixture_writer.go re-implements the canonical envelope, commit header and SSHSIG layout, mitigated in-head by VerifySSHCommit at the end of Prepare and by every sample recomputing a trusted digest through an ordinary app.Open plus Snapshot.\n\nSECURITY\n\nSound, and unchanged outside the harness. The typed cache key removes a parser rather than adding one: fixtureKey is now a comparable struct used directly as a map key, so the NUL-joined string and its strconv.Atoi decode are gone, and with them the only place in this path that could mis-parse a key. The manifest comparison is unchanged in substance and still refuses on any of generator version, seed, shape, depth or actor count. The decoded-result comparison is a pure integer equality on a value already parsed by the JSON decoder; it introduces no new input, no allocation bound and no external call, and it fails closed by returning an error that aborts the lane rather than warning. Fixture inputs are unchanged. Production submission and verification authority are untouched: nothing outside cmd/gitseq-perf changed in this delta. My round 1 security conclusion stands, including the scoped, intentional relaxation that rewriteConfig clears VerifiedFrontier in the throwaway materialized sample.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION\n\nThe typed struct key is a genuine simplification, not a relabelling. It deletes both fixtureKey(shape, actorCount) and parseFixtureKey, removes an error return and its unreachable-by-construction error path from ensureFixtures, and lets the key be constructed once by runCase.fixtureKey and used at both the ensure and the lookup site, so the two can no longer disagree. That is a net reduction in code and in failure modes, and it is what I asked for in round 1.\n\nNo further cut is warranted here. One cosmetic note only, not worth a change on its own: the type fixtureKey and the method runCase.fixtureKey now share a name. Go keeps them in separate namespaces so this is unambiguous to the compiler, and renaming either would churn the call sites for a readability gain I do not think is worth it at this head.\n\nGATES at 7ab18c6b\n\ngo build ./... exit 0. go vet ./... exit 0. go test ./... -count=1 gives 24 packages ok and one failure, TestPublicRepositorySurface, which is the separately asserted README base failure 92f89b90. It is not this head's: the branch touches none of README.md, SECURITY.md or public_surface_test.go, and main is still ab524c3f, so the parallel disclosure repair at a1680d71 has not landed yet and the failure persists exactly as in round 1. Treated as separate. The gated suite with GITSEQ_PERF_INTEGRATION=1 passes across internal/perfscenario, internal/perflane and cmd/gitseq-perf, and both new regressions pass on their own.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59514c7588eba9fa4c8c8bc7d5b797984b803c86","head":"7ab18c6be3fa54ad7bae46ac4565b21d9d89081e","verdict":"approved"}}